Finding: {"title":"Processing fee and dollar-amount conversion uses floating point arithmetic, risking off-by-a-cent charges","severity":"MEDIUM","area":"Money math correctness","files":["src/server/routes/routes-micamp.ts"],"detail":"L476-481: `processingFee = Math.round(amount * (feePercent / 100))` is fine (rounds to integer cents), but `amountDollars = totalCharged / 100` performs floating-point division that can produce values like `19.990000000000002` for `totalCharged = 1999`. This value is sent directly to MiCamp as `Amount` (micamp.ts `Sale`/`Refund`/`saleWithToken` request bodies) without any `.toFixed(2)` normalization — relying on JSON.stringify + the downstream JSON parser/gateway to coerce it correctly. Similarly `refundAmountDollars = refundAmountCents / 100` at L695 has the same issue.","impact":"Most cent values convert cleanly, but certain amounts are documented IEEE-754 cases that don't round-trip cleanly through `/100`, risking a malformed amount being sent to a live payment gateway (either rejected, or charged as a slightly different amount than displayed to the user/recorded in totalCharged).","recommendation":"Normalize dollar amounts sent to MiCamp with `Number((cents / 100).toFixed(2))` (or format as a fixed 2-decimal string if the API accepts one) before sending, both in routes-micamp.ts and centrally inside micamp.ts's sale/refund/saleWithToken methods so every caller gets the guard.","confidence":"medium"}
confirmed=true only if the defect genuinely exists. If misread → verdict="false-positive". If real but wrong severity → set correctedSeverity. If real but underspecified → verdict="needs-detail" (kept). Return JSON per the schema.

